The restrictive separator follows firi.hpp's ellipsoid-metric tangent
principle, but uses a new finite 2D angular active-set enumeration for a
complete convex cell and two clearance-protected seed endpoints. It maximizes
signed cell support divided by the ellipsoidal normal norm, then subtracts
metric clearance. Unlike the former positive-offset SLSQP representation,
its offset is free and negative support does not exclude a feasible separator.
Stationary directions, cell-support switches and clearance-active directions
exhaust the angular extrema; no optimizer retries or point-cloud replacement
are used. The existing certified NLopt MVIE iteration remains unchanged.
This is an attributed whole-cell adaptation, not unchanged upstream FIRI or
a claim of globally maximum-volume polygons.

No lbfgs.hpp or sdlp.hpp code is incorporated. The inspected sdlp.hpp has separate Hohmeyer (1990) and Wang (2021) permissive notices; lbfgs.hpp has no file-level license notice. Neither is needed: the Python reference uses NumPy/SciPy; the C++ runtime uses installed Eigen/NLopt.

Original-edge subdivision and shared-endpoint connection regions adapt the
convexCover mechanism in gcopter/include/gcopter/sfc_gen.hpp at the same pinned
commit. Unlike upstream, every solver result and adjacent interior overlap is
checked; obstacles are complete grid cells with metric clearance, not points.
Post-generation pruning adapts shortCut's backward earliest-predecessor search
from that same file. Unlike upstream's overlap-only shortcut, every skipped
span must retain continuous original-edge coverage by analytic halfspace
clipping, and every final join retains the certified interior witness. No
ignored solver failure return is incorporated.
